#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccdd6eb6-30fa-580d-92a3-888c20d92865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{64-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317679} {:answer/id #uuid "e2368140-39a2-5d26-a5a7-5c4c57975e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} {:answer/id #uuid "c5ea363a-f4d3-54d3-bc2a-9ffbd48302c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317681} {:answer/id #uuid "0184e5c6-1bf0-590c-9ece-2e819ba361e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317682} {:answer/id #uuid "9234e108-cc9b-5b79-a281-a9e3e1a74665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2368140-39a2-5d26-a5a7-5c4c57975e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317680} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc59459f-1e90-54cd-84d9-f3f11e7a1a71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b60038c6-cb0f-5d07-b76f-fbce9c0d51b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122339" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $0\\,m$ when its velocity is $-2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e007c7-6cc3-5e98-99e9-dc4b2cdd07ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317693} {:answer/id #uuid "64345185-5abe-5170-9e41-b6e3f42d7d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317694} {:answer/id #uuid "fe150ef1-3540-5259-be8b-b205591045c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317695} {:answer/id #uuid "3db94eb2-4153-5552-b898-d5c7e4d753b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317696} {:answer/id #uuid "9dae4abf-bd8d-5985-8531-89977efe3c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64345185-5abe-5170-9e41-b6e3f42d7d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317694} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2f47d5-7302-5cb4-88e6-fef841d5bf39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7832c005-e5d4-5bbe-91b5-55956ce35ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-122342" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042763/assets/4e0b8b816ca3b86fa90ea785df1308f53a4763baa2c2aa2192b9d7bfa0259855.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4{x}^{3}+4x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int (4{x}^{3}+4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{6})^{2} & ={1}^{4}+2(1)^{2}+C \\\\ 3 & =3+C \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{4}+2{x}^{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{2({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{2({2}^{4}+2(2)^{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
